1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,200
What if your money had a hard limit?

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:06,000
Not a soft limit.
Not a government policy.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,500
Not a promise.
A mathematical guarantee.

4
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:11,500
Enforced by code.

5
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:15,000
21 million. No more. Ever.

6
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Today we understand how Bitcoin
and Ethereum actually work.

7
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
And why they solve
completely different problems.

8
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Digital money is not new.
PayPal launched in 1998.

9
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Online banking existed before that.

10
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,000
You could move numbers electronically
for decades before Bitcoin.

11
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,500
So what did Bitcoin actually change?

12
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:40,000
Three things.

13
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,500
First: no issuer.
No company creates or controls Bitcoin.

14
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:47,000
The protocol runs itself.

15
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Second: no gatekeeper.
Any person with internet can transact.

16
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,000
No account required.
No approval needed. No ID check.

17
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Third: a fixed supply.
No entity can create more Bitcoin.

18
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:03,000
The total supply is 21 million.

19
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,500
Written into the code in 2009.

20
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:10,000
Think about regular money.
A central bank controls the supply.

21
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:14,000
When an economy needs stimulus,
more money is printed.

22
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,000
When inflation runs high,
policy changes.

23
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,000
The rules are set by humans
and changed by humans.

24
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Bitcoin's rules are set by math.

25
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,000
The code IS the policy.

26
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:34,000
And changing it requires every node
in the network to agree.

27
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:38,500
That has effectively never happened
for the core rules.

28
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:43,000
So who actually adds new blocks
to the Bitcoin chain?

29
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:47,000
Specialized computers called miners.

30
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Here is how it works.

31
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Every 10 minutes, miners compete
to solve a puzzle.

32
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:00,000
The puzzle: find a number
called a nonce.

33
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Such that, when combined with
the block's data and hashed —

34
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:09,000
the output starts with
a specific number of zeros.

35
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
There is no shortcut.
There is no formula.

36
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,000
You can only find it by trying
billions of random guesses per second.

37
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:22,000
The first miner to find a valid solution
broadcasts the new block.

38
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Every other node verifies it instantly.

39
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:30,000
The winner gets a reward: new Bitcoin.

40
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,000
This mechanism is called
Proof of Work.

41
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Why work? Because computation
costs real energy.

42
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,000
That energy cost is the security model.

43
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Think of it like this.

44
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:51,000
Imagine a combination lock
with a billion possible combinations.

45
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,000
The only way to open it
is to try every number.

46
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:59,000
That takes time.
That takes resources.

47
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:04,000
Anyone who wants to attack the network
must out-compute all honest miners.

48
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:09,000
Today, that would cost millions
of dollars per hour.

49
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:13,000
That is the anchor.

50
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:18,000
Bitcoin's network currently uses roughly
150 terawatt-hours per year.

51
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:23,000
That sounds enormous. And it is.

52
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:28,000
But an attacker must match
that entire energy budget.

53
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Sustainably — to have any chance.

54
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Let's talk about where
Bitcoin comes from.

55
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:43,000
When a miner wins the puzzle
and adds a block,

56
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:47,000
they receive a reward.
New Bitcoin, created by the protocol.

57
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:51,000
This is the only way
new Bitcoin enters circulation.

58
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:55,000
In 2009, the reward was
50 BTC per block.

59
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Every 210,000 blocks —
roughly every four years —

60
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:04,000
that reward is cut in half.
This is called a halving.

61
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:08,500
2009: 50 BTC per block.
2012: 25 BTC.

62
00:04:08,500 --> 00:04:13,000
2016: 12.5 BTC.
2020: 6.25 BTC.

63
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:17,500
2024: 3.125 BTC.

64
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:22,000
The halvings continue until the reward
reaches a number so small —

65
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:26,500
it rounds to zero.
That happens around the year 2140.

66
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:31,000
At that point, no new Bitcoin
will ever be created.

67
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:35,500
Total supply: 21 million. Final.

68
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:40,000
As of 2024, about 19.7 million Bitcoin
have already been mined.

69
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Only 1.3 million remain.

70
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:49,000
Think of it like a gold mine
where the vein gets thinner by half

71
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,000
every four years.
By design. Built into the bedrock.

72
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:57,000
No negotiation. No exception.

73
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:02,000
This predictable scarcity is one reason
Bitcoin is compared to gold.

74
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:07,000
But unlike gold, you can verify
the total supply of Bitcoin in seconds.

75
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:11,500
Run a node. Count the coins.
Transparent and auditable by anyone.

76
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:17,000
Bitcoin answered one question:
how do we move value without a bank?

77
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:22,000
In 2013, a 19-year-old
named Vitalik Buterin

78
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:26,000
asked a different question.

79
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:30,500
What if we could run code
without a server?

80
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:35,000
His insight: a blockchain is not a ledger.

81
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:39,500
It is a shared, unstoppable computer.

82
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:44,000
Bitcoin's blockchain can record:
'Alex sent 1 BTC to Maria.'

83
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:49,000
Ethereum's blockchain can record
and execute:

84
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:54,000
'IF Maria deposits 100 ETH,
THEN release the loan.'

85
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:59,000
'IF she misses a payment,
THEN liquidate automatically.'

86
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:03,500
This is the fundamental difference.
Bitcoin: a currency system.

87
00:06:03,500 --> 00:06:08,000
Ethereum: a programmable platform.

88
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:12,500
The programs that run on Ethereum
are called smart contracts.

89
00:06:12,500 --> 00:06:17,000
They live on the blockchain.
They run automatically.

90
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:21,000
No company behind them.
No server to switch off.

91
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:26,000
Think of it this way.
Bitcoin is a vending machine.

92
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:30,500
You put money in. You get something out.
That is all it does.

93
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:35,000
Ethereum is a general-purpose computer.

94
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:40,000
You can run any program on it —
lending, trading, voting, identity.

95
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:45,000
As long as you can write the logic.

96
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:50,000
Vitalik launched Ethereum in 2015.

97
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:55,000
Today, tens of thousands of applications
run on it.

98
00:06:55,000 --> 00:07:00,000
None of them require permission
from Ethereum to exist.

99
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:05,000
In September 2022, Ethereum did something
Bitcoin has never done.

100
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:09,500
It changed its consensus mechanism.

101
00:07:09,500 --> 00:07:13,500
This event was called The Merge.

102
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:18,000
Ethereum switched from Proof of Work
to Proof of Stake.

103
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:22,500
In Proof of Work, miners compete
using energy.

104
00:07:22,500 --> 00:07:27,000
In Proof of Stake, validators compete
using collateral.

105
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:31,500
Here is how Proof of Stake works.

106
00:07:31,500 --> 00:07:36,000
To become a validator,
you lock up 32 ETH as a deposit.

107
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:40,000
This is called staking.

108
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:44,500
The protocol randomly selects validators
to propose new blocks.

109
00:07:44,500 --> 00:07:49,000
If you propose valid blocks honestly —
you earn rewards.

110
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:54,000
If you cheat, try to include
invalid transactions, or go offline —

111
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:58,000
part of your deposit is destroyed.
This is called slashing.

112
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:02,500
Your locked ETH is the security.
Cheating means losing money directly.

113
00:08:02,500 --> 00:08:07,000
That financial stake replaces
the energy cost.

114
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:12,000
The result: Ethereum's energy use
dropped 99.9% after The Merge.

115
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:17,000
The same security guarantees —
enforced by economic punishment.

116
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:22,000
Instead of computation.

117
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:27,000
Proof of Work: prove you did the work
by showing the energy bill.

118
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:31,500
And the blisters.

119
00:08:31,500 --> 00:08:36,000
Proof of Stake: prove your commitment
by putting your own money first.

120
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:41,000
One of the most common misconceptions:
a crypto wallet holds your Bitcoin.

121
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:45,000
It does not.

122
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:49,500
Your Bitcoin lives on the blockchain.

123
00:08:49,500 --> 00:08:54,000
What a wallet holds is the key
that proves you own it.

124
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:58,500
Here is the three-layer structure.

125
00:08:58,500 --> 00:09:03,000
Private key.
A 256-bit number. Randomly generated.

126
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:07,000
This is your proof of ownership.

127
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:11,500
Whoever holds the private key
controls the coins.

128
00:09:11,500 --> 00:09:16,000
Lose it — the coins are inaccessible.
Forever.

129
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:20,000
Share it — the coins are gone.

130
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:24,500
Public key.
Mathematically derived from the private key.

131
00:09:24,500 --> 00:09:29,000
This derivation is one-way.

132
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:33,000
You can generate the public key
from the private key.

133
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:37,000
You cannot go backwards.

134
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:41,500
Address.
A shorter version, derived from the public key.

135
00:09:41,500 --> 00:09:46,000
This is what you share when someone
wants to send you Bitcoin.

136
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:50,000
Like a mailbox number.

137
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:55,000
When you send Bitcoin:
you create a transaction.

138
00:09:55,000 --> 00:10:00,000
From address A, to address B, amount X.

139
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:04,500
You sign it with your private key.

140
00:10:04,500 --> 00:10:09,000
The signature proves ownership
without revealing the private key.

141
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:13,500
The transaction is broadcast
to the network.

142
00:10:13,500 --> 00:10:17,500
Nodes verify the signature.
If valid — included in the next block.

143
00:10:17,500 --> 00:10:22,000
The analogy:
your address is your mailbox.

144
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:26,500
Anyone can find it.
Anyone can drop something in.

145
00:10:26,500 --> 00:10:31,000
Your private key is a master seal stamp.

146
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:35,500
Only you can authorize
an outgoing package.

147
00:10:35,500 --> 00:10:40,000
The signature on the package
proves it came from you —

148
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:44,000
without exposing the stamp itself.

149
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:49,000
Here is what you now understand.

150
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:54,000
Bitcoin: a fixed-supply digital currency.
21 million maximum.

151
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:58,500
Secured by Proof of Work.

152
00:10:58,500 --> 00:11:03,000
Ethereum: a programmable blockchain.
Secured by Proof of Stake since 2022.

153
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:08,000
Smart contracts: code that runs
automatically on Ethereum.

154
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:12,000
No company required.

155
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:17,000
Wallets: key holders, not coin holders.
Private key equals ownership.

156
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:22,000
These are the two foundational protocols.

157
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:27,000
Everything else in this space
builds on top of them.

158
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:32,000
In Phase 3, we go into DeFi.

159
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:37,000
Decentralized lending, trading, and yield —
all running on smart contracts.

160
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:41,500
No banks. No brokers. No permission.

161
00:11:41,500 --> 00:11:46,000
Subscribe now.
Phase 3 is already scheduled.
